=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The user's report that "router4 is down" is incorrect. Router4 is fully operational: its gateway interface (eth0, 192.168.200.1/24) is UP, and hosts PC11-PC14 can ping it successfully at 0% loss. The actual fault is a misconfigured firewall ACL on **router6**.

**Affected component:** The iptables/nftables FORWARD chain on router6 (the gateway router for the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet hosting PC15-PC18).

**Mechanism:** Router6's nftables `filter` table contains a FORWARD chain with four rules applied specifically to traffic ingressing on `eth2` (the WAN link 10.50.1.0/24 connecting to router3): (1) ACCEPT TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 only; (2) DROP all TCP; (3) DROP all UDP; (4) DROP everything else including ICMP. Because all inter-subnet traffic returning to 172.18.51.0/24 from the rest of the network enters router6 via eth2, it is silently dropped before reaching PC15-PC18. Outbound packets from PC15-PC18 leave via eth1→eth2 and are not blocked, but since replies are dropped on the way back, all sessions time out.

**Localization:** router6, eth2 (r6_r3 link, 10.50.1.2/24), nftables FORWARD chain.

**Root cause:** An overly restrictive nftables ACL on router6 drops all forwarded traffic entering from the inter-router WAN interface (eth2), isolating the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet from the entire rest of the network.

**Fix:** Remove (or correct) the DROP rules in the nftables FORWARD chain on router6 — specifically the three blanket DROP rules matching `iifname "eth2"` — to restore bidirectional forwarding between the 172.18.51.0/24 LAN and all other subnets.
=== END ===